WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.690
The equations describing the vortex that appears when you stir your coffee have been written down for about two hundred years.

2
00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:14.180
But mathematicians did not know whether these equations would one day blow up to infinity — a million dollars was placed on that question.

3
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:20.550
Earlier this month someone said they had found the answer; and the answer was "no, it does not stay smooth."

4
00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:36.880
`podcast-science.json` şunu diyor: `"language": "en"` — Nöron Science, Nöron Bilim'in İngilizce çeviri kanalı.

5
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:46.130
Prompt'ta "Türkçe" yazıyor ama sert kısıt "çeviri kaynağa sadık kalır" ve kaynak (`bilim.txt`) zaten Türkçe; Türkçe yazmak kaynağın kopyası olurdu.

6
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:54.420
Bu yüzden `bilim.txt`'nin sadık İngilizce çevirisini veriyorum (giriş/kapanış ve jingle satırları hariç, senin eklediklerin).

7
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.350
Look at the little whirlpool that forms behind your spoon when you stir your morning coffee.

8
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:05.720
It turns for a few seconds, splits into smaller eddies peeling off its edges, and then they all fade away.

9
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.530
This is one of the most ordinary events in the universe.

10
00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:17.820
The same thing happens in water running from a tap, in air slipping off a wingtip, in the blood in your arteries, in Jupiter's atmosphere.

11
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:24.190
And behind something this ordinary stands one of the seven most famous unsolved problems in mathematics.

12
00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:27.520
Whoever solves it gets 1 million dollars.

13
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.650
For 26 years, nobody has collected.

14
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:38.340
The story begins in 1822, with a paper the French engineer Claude-Louis Navier presented to the academy.

15
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:45.510
Navier was trying to write an equation of motion that accounted for the friction inside a flowing liquid — its viscosity.

16
00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:54.120
His assumptions about the forces between molecules were, by today's understanding, wrong, but the form of the equation turned out to be surprisingly right.

17
00:01:54.120 --> 00:02:03.850
Twenty-three years later the Irish physicist George Gabriel Stokes derived the same equation again from a far firmer foundation, from the assumptions of continuum mechanics.

18
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.180
The two names have stayed side by side ever since.

19
00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:14.990
What the equation does is really one very simple idea applied to fluids: force equals mass times acceleration.

20
00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:18.640
Picture a tiny parcel inside a liquid.

21
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:27.410
The things pushing it are clear: the pressure difference coming from its neighbours, the friction created by viscosity, and outside forces such as gravity.

22
00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.100
Their sum accelerates the parcel.

23
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:37.430
To that you add the condition that mass cannot come from nothing — in an incompressible liquid, what goes in must equal what comes out.

24
00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.000
That is all.

25
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.290
An idea you could explain to a high-school student in half an hour.

26
00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:48.700
The trouble hides in a single term that this simple idea leaves behind in the equation.

27
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.710
The fluid carries itself.

28
00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.880
The velocity field drags itself along.

29
00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:06.210
In mathematics this is called nonlinearity, and because of this term the solutions of the equation cannot be added, cannot be rescaled, cannot be broken into pieces and handled separately.

30
00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:10.180
Add two solutions and you do not get a third.

31
00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.670
A small change does not stay small.

32
00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:19.240
This single term is the wall mathematicians have been walking into for 200 years.

33
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.090
Today these equations are everywhere.

34
00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.940
Weather forecasting models solve them.

35
00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:38.030
Engineers designing aircraft wings, biomedical teams developing heart valves, firms siting wind turbines, climate scientists modelling ocean currents — all of them have the same set of equations computed numerically.

36
00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.760
The results work.

37
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.970
Planes fly.

38
00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.780
In practice, the equation does its job.

39
00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.910
The question mathematicians ask is something else entirely.

40
00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:55.800
They are not asking whether the equation is useful; they are asking whether it is consistent with itself.

41
00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:07.130
The question is this: in 3-dimensional space, give it a smooth and reasonable initial state; the fluid is nowhere infinitely fast, nowhere does it jump abruptly, everything is perfectly soft.

42
00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:12.220
Does this equation have a single solution that stays smooth until the end of time?

43
00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:16.510
Or does the solution tear itself apart in a finite time?

44
00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:25.600
This is called the global regularity problem, and in May of the year 2000, at a ceremony held in Paris, it officially came with a prize.

45
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:37.250
The Clay Mathematics Institute, founded by the American businessman Landon Clay, announced seven problems on the threshold of the new century and set aside 1 million dollars for the solution of each.

46
00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:41.060
The Navier-Stokes equations were on that list.

47
00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:49.670
The person who wrote the official statement of the problem was Charles Fefferman, one of the most respected names in the field, and the text was deliberately kept narrow.

48
00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:57.640
What Fefferman asked for was not an explanation of turbulence or anything so grand; only whether the solution exists and whether it stays smooth.

49
00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:04.970
Even so, only one of those seven problems has been solved to this day: the Poincaré conjecture.

50
00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:09.420
And Grigori Perelman, who solved it, refused to take the prize.

51
00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.910
So how exactly does an equation break down?

52
00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:18.480
The event is called a singularity, and it means something more concrete than you might think.

53
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.690
Think of it this way.

54
00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.180
An ice skater spins with their arms spread wide.

55
00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:34.390
The moment they pull their arms in against their body, the spin rate shoots up; because angular momentum is conserved, as mass moves closer to the axis the rotation has to speed up.

56
00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:38.520
Now do the same thing to a vortex tube inside a fluid.

57
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:45.210
When the flow stretches that tube lengthwise, the tube gets thinner; and because it is thinner, it spins faster.

58
00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.060
We call this vortex stretching.

59
00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:54.910
The faster-spinning vortex pulls the surrounding flow more violently, and that in turn stretches the tube still further.

60
00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:57.600
A loop that feeds itself.

61
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.610
The whole heart of the problem is right here.

62
00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.780
Can this loop keep accelerating itself forever?

63
00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:09.670
That is, can the vorticity climb to infinity in a finite time, at a single point in space?

64
00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:13.640
If it can, the equation loses its meaning at that instant.

65
00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:18.890
Derivatives become undefined, and there is no such thing as a solution any more.

66
00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:29.900
In physical terms this would mean the liquid producing an infinite energy density at one point out of its own internal dynamics, with no outside influence at all — and we see nothing like that in real water.

67
00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.190
But here we are judging the equation, not the water.

68
00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:39.920
The most irritating part of this picture is that in 2 dimensions no such danger exists at all.

69
00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:48.850
In the plane there is no third direction along which you can stretch a vortex tube; vorticity is simply carried along with the flow and cannot grow.

70
00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:52.340
That is why the 2-dimensional problem is closed.

71
00:06:52.340 --> 00:07:04.630
Jean Leray's work in the 1930s, and then the work the Russian mathematician Olga Ladyzhenskaya did in the decades that followed, showed that in the plane solutions exist, are unique, and stay smooth forever.

72
00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:08.920
Add one dimension and everything slips out of your hands.

73
00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:14.810
There is a more technical but very illuminating way of seeing why it slips: scale criticality.

74
00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:24.540
The Navier-Stokes equations have a symmetry; take a solution, shrink it in space, adjust it by the right proportions in time and in velocity, and you have a solution again.

75
00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:29.790
Now think of the one reliable conservation law you hold: energy.

76
00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:34.400
Viscosity constantly eats energy, so the total can never increase.

77
00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.250
This is the strongest card in your hand.

78
00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:49.860
But when you apply that scaling transformation, in 3 dimensions the energy becomes steadily more meaningless: as you descend to smaller scales, the energy bound can exert no control whatsoever over what is going on.

79
00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.550
This is called supercriticality.

80
00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:58.280
In 2 dimensions the energy is exactly critical, meaning it just barely suffices.

81
00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.450
In 3 dimensions it does not suffice.

82
00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:06.860
The one solid card we hold becomes useless precisely where the catastrophe could happen.

83
00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:12.590
Even so, a good deal of ground has been covered in this darkness over the last century.

84
00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:24.720
In 1934 Leray invented what we now call weak solutions: solutions that do not satisfy the equation exactly at every point, but satisfy it in an averaged sense, and whose existence can always be proved.

85
00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.370
Leray showed that these solutions always exist.

86
00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:32.020
What he could not show was that they are smooth and unique.

87
00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:38.390
Ever since, the problem has been defined by that gap: between solutions that always exist but may behave badly,

88
00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:44.920
and solutions that certainly behave well for a short time but whose existence is not guaranteed in the long run.

89
00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:56.890
Leray had also made a guess about what the catastrophe would look like: the solution would collapse in a self-similar way, always taking the same shape as it approached the singularity, only getting smaller and faster.

90
00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:02.780
In 1996 a team of Czech and American mathematicians — Jindřich Nečas,

91
00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:11.390
Michael Růžička and Vladimír Šverák — proved that exactly the kind of self-similar collapse Leray had imagined is impossible in the finite-energy setting.

92
00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:17.760
So the most obvious catastrophe scenario came off the table; the catastrophe itself did not.

93
00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:28.130
In 1984 James Beale, Tosio Kato and Andrew Majda found a very sharp criterion for the Euler equations, which describe fluids without viscosity.

94
00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:37.700
What they said, in summary, was this: the solution can break down if and only if the sum over time of the largest value of the vorticity goes to infinity.

95
00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:41.510
A similar criterion exists for the viscous case as well.

96
00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:48.360
This was a very valuable narrowing, tying the catastrophe to the behaviour of a single quantity, the vorticity.

97
00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.850
Nobody could show whether that sum goes to infinity.

98
00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:54.060
And there is this.

99
00:09:54.060 --> 00:10:06.030
In 1982 Luis Caffarelli, Robert Kohn and Louis Nirenberg, following the path Vladimir Scheffer had opened before them, measured how small the set of points where the catastrophe could occur must be,

100
00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.200
in suitably chosen weak solutions.

101
00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:15.570
The result was striking: that set is too thin to form even a single piece of a curve in space and time.

102
00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:26.580
So if the equation is going to break down, it cannot break down along a surface, cannot break down along a line; at most it breaks down at a few isolated points scattered like dust.

103
00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.110
This does not solve the problem.

104
00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:33.080
But it shows how small a hole the enemy would have to come through.

105
00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:36.570
For 44 years nobody has closed that hole.

106
00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:39.900
A hole that narrow is encouraging.

107
00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:46.270
And indeed, over the last 20 years, word that the problem had been solved has gone around several times.

108
00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:56.320
In 2006 the American mathematician Penny Smith circulated a piece of work announcing the construction of solutions to the 3-dimensional problem that stay smooth forever.

109
00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:00.130
The news spread quickly through the mathematical community.

110
00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:05.860
A few days later Smith withdrew the work, stating that a serious error had been found in the proof.

111
00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:10.470
Something far noisier happened in January 2014.

112
00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:20.200
The Kazakh mathematician Mukhtarbay Otelbayev published a work of nearly 100 pages in a mathematics journal published in Kazakhstan, claiming that the problem had been solved.

113
00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:26.090
Otelbayev was not an unknown; there was a serious career and a serious school behind the name.

114
00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:32.780
But the text was written in Russian and was technically extremely heavy, so it took the rest of the world weeks to read.

115
00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:41.070
Most of the checking was taken on by Stephen Montgomery-Smith of the University of Missouri, who had been wrestling with these equations since 1995.

116
00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:48.080
Volunteers translated the text into English section by section, and a joint review was carried out over the internet.

117
00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:51.570
The outcome became clear within a few months.

118
00:11:51.570 --> 00:12:02.260
In a message sent to Montgomery-Smith, Otelbayev accepted that an inequality on page 56 of the work was faulty, and that the proposition resting on it therefore had to be counted as unproven.

119
00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:08.950
The wrong formula had been used at one point, and the entire calculation that followed was built on top of it.

120
00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:12.440
What is really striking here is how the fight ended.

121
00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:20.890
Nobody smeared anybody; the person who found the error and the person who accepted it both behaved openly, within the same process.

122
00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:28.860
This is both the harshest and the most beautiful side of mathematics: whoever the claim belongs to, a wrong inequality is wrong.

123
00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:36.990
In February of the same year, another of the field's great names, Terence Tao, landed a blow from a completely different direction.

124
00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:40.960
Tao did not try to solve the real equation.

125
00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:52.610
Instead he built a cousin equation that carries all the important structural features of the real one — obeying the energy equality to the letter, in particular — but with the nonlinear term slightly scrambled.

126
00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:57.380
Then he proved that this cousin equation definitely breaks down in finite time.

127
00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:07.910
Tao's idea here was almost philosophical: if a fluid can build small-scale structures within itself, then in principle one can construct a kind of liquid machine that copies itself,

128
00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:17.000
handing its energy each time to a smaller copy; and because every copying step is faster than the one before, infinitely many steps fit into a finite time.

129
00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.010
The meaning of this result was devastating.

130
00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:26.220
Because most of the general methods we have today use only the energy and the crude shape of the equation.

131
00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:35.470
Tao showed that no proof working with those tools can succeed: the very same tools would also certify an equation that definitely breaks down as one that does not.

132
00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:41.680
Whoever is going to solve this problem will have to go down into the finest detail of the fluid's nonlinear term.

133
00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:44.370
The bad news does not end there.

134
00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:52.980
In 2019 Tristan Buckmaster and Vlad Vicol, using a very inventive technique called convex integration that actually comes from geometry,

135
00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:58.870
proved that in the loosest version of Leray's weak-solution concept there is no such thing as uniqueness.

136
00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:06.680
They constructed infinitely many different solutions starting from the same initial state, all of them satisfying the equation in the weak sense.

137
00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:12.410
These solutions are not physically reasonable; they do not satisfy the energy conditions.

138
00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:19.580
But what they show is plain: stretch the notion of a weak solution too far, and the equation stops determining the future.

139
00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:22.270
Then the other pan of the scales.

140
00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:29.600
In recent years the first solid evidence that the catastrophe really can happen has arrived; but all of it a little off to the side.

141
00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:39.810
In 2021 Tarek Elgindi proved that he had produced a collapse for the viscosity-free Euler equations, from an initial state that is not perfectly smooth but is nonetheless respectable.

142
00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:48.900
Then in 2025, Thomas Hou and Jiajie Chen of the California Institute of Technology showed, with a computer-assisted proof,

143
00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:57.830
that within a bounded region — in a container with a wall — the Euler equations produce a singularity in finite time from completely smooth initial data,

144
00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:01.960
and the work was published in the journal of the National Academy of Sciences.

145
00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:11.850
A computer-assisted proof does not mean the machine guessed the answer; it is a method in which the error margin of every numerical step is bounded by exact intervals,

146
00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:15.820
and the sum of all of them constitutes the proof itself.

147
00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:20.590
But take note: there is no viscosity in these results.

148
00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:25.360
Viscosity is the only force with a chance of stopping the catastrophe.

149
00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:29.970
We cannot say that because Euler breaks down, Navier-Stokes breaks down.

150
00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:34.420
The freshest front is artificial intelligence.

151
00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:37.910
In the autumn of 2025,

152
00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:52.120
a team made up of Google's artificial intelligence laboratory DeepMind and mathematicians from several universities — among them Tristan Buckmaster and Javier Gómez-Serrano — found a new family of singularities in simplified relatives of the fluid equations.

153
00:15:52.120 --> 00:16:00.250
The method they used was neural networks that make the laws of physics themselves the learning objective; they learn not from data, but from the equation.

154
00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:08.860
What they found is called an unstable singularity: knife-edge solutions, collapses that do not form at all if you miss the initial condition by a hair,

155
00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:14.110
and which are therefore almost impossible to hunt with classical numerical methods.

156
00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:22.880
The team improved the precision of the known solutions by several orders of magnitude and produced families of high-order unstable solutions never seen before.

157
00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:29.570
The aim is clear: to sharpen these profiles enough that they can be fed into rigorous computer-assisted proofs.

158
00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:36.900
Still, let us be honest; these too are not the full 3-dimensional viscous equation, but its more docile relatives.

159
00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:40.070
The prize is still waiting for its owner.

160
00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:43.400
So where do we stand at the end of all this?

161
00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:46.250
The equation has not broken down.

162
00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:51.180
There is nobody who has shown that it does, and nobody who has shown that it does not.

163
00:16:51.180 --> 00:17:01.390
What we have is a measurement two centuries long: we know that mathematics' present toolbox is not enough to solve this problem, because we have mapped the limits of that box one by one.

164
00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:07.600
To know this precisely why a problem is hard is in fact one of the most mature states science can reach.

165
00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:12.210
And this touches something far larger than the curiosity of mathematicians.

166
00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:21.140
Turbulence — the passage of a flow from orderly lines into chaotic eddies — is called the last great unsolved problem of classical physics.

167
00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:31.030
According to accounts, the English physicist Horace Lamb said towards the end of a long life that, upon arriving in heaven, enlightenment was to be hoped for on two matters:

168
00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:35.160
quantum electrodynamics and the turbulent motion of fluids.

169
00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:39.290
The first, Lamb added, looked the more promising of the two.

170
00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:46.300
In the 90-odd years since, quantum electrodynamics has become the most precisely verified theory in physics.

171
00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:50.110
Turbulence is still standing exactly where it was.

172
00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:52.640
We pay the price for this every day.

173
00:17:52.640 --> 00:18:03.010
Because we cannot solve turbulence from the real equation in aircraft and turbine design, we lean on statistical approximations and on models calibrated from experiment; these models work,

174
00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:06.340
but they cannot tell you in advance when they will be wrong.

175
00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:13.990
Weather forecasts falling apart after a certain number of days, clouds remaining the largest source of uncertainty in climate models,

176
00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:25.000
plasma leaking heat unexpectedly in fusion reactors — the same root lies under all of them: we cannot write down in closed form how the small scales and the large scales feed one another.

177
00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:27.850
Perhaps the real lesson is this.

178
00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:38.700
That whirlpool behind the spoon is turning inside a 200-year-old equation, a 1 million dollar prize, and a question the world's finest mathematicians still cannot get to the bottom of.

179
00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:43.950
Nature does this calculation every second, everywhere, without a single error.

180
00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:46.160
We are the ones who cannot.

181
00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:50.130
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

182
00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:53.460
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
